      "topic": "Differentiation - Stationary points and function behavior",
      "subpart_details": [
        {
          "subpart": "a",
          "marks": 4,
          "syllabus_outcomes": ["1.7"],
          "answers": {
            "answer_snippet": "dy/dx = -k(3x - k)^(-2) × 3 + 3. Set dy/dx = 0: -3k/(3x - k)² + 3 = 0 leading to (3x - k)² = k. x = (k ± √k)/3"
          }
        },
        {
          "subpart": "b",
          "marks": 3,
          "syllabus_outcomes": ["1.7"],
          "answers": {
            "answer_snippet": "Substitute x = a when k = 4: a = (4 ± √4)/3 leading to a = 2. f''(x) = -12(3x - 4)^(-3) × 3 = 18k(3x - k)^(-3). At x = 2: f''(2) = 72(3 × 2 - 4)^(-3) = 72 > 0 (or 9) when x = 2, hence minimum"
          }
        },
        {
          "subpart": "c",
          "marks": 2,
          "syllabus_outcomes": ["1.7"],
          "answers": {
            "answer_snippet": "g'(x) = 3/(3x + 1)² + 3. When k = -1, stationary point equation has no solutions, so g is increasing or decreasing. Show g'(x) is positive for any value of x (= 3/[(3x + 1)²] + 3 > 0), hence g is an increasing function"
          }
        }
      ]
